P18-V01 nonpositive decoder field parity and local gate refresh — 2026-10-22 20:11 UTC

Environment: macOS AArch64, Apple M5 Max (18 logical CPUs), Rust 1.85.0.
Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`. Pinned RocketMQ Java
client/source: 5.5.1, commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.

Implementation commit: `8ca83fc8 fix(common): accept nonpositive decoder field
lengths`. Pinned Java `MessageDecoder.decode(ByteBuffer)` only reads body or
property bytes for positive lengths. Zero and negative body lengths leave the
body absent; zero and negative property lengths leave the property map absent.
In both cases Java's buffer position remains immediately after the length
field. Rust now matches these cases and returns the matching consumed-byte
count. The Java fixture records a negative body case with body bytes removed
and a negative property case with the original property bytes unconsumed. The
fixture SHA-256 is
`9cd0b64fe95643177c533d6cc2fd1f33c957e54cd3bd9559ecb07f6083939938`.

Harness notes: the first Java compile found a duplicate local fixture variable;
renamed it and recompiled. The first Common differential run caught an obsolete
expectation that negative properties fail; updated the regression to assert
Java's absent-map result and cursor. The regenerated fixture then passed Maven
verification.

